<p>
  There are two completely different problems hiding inside "how do I get
  customers". Getting the first five is one problem. Getting a fiftieth is a
  different one. Almost all advice online is about the second, which is why it is
  useless when you are at zero.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">The first five are people, not an audience</h2>

<p>
  You do not need marketing to get five customers. You need to talk to about
  thirty people, which sounds worse and is much easier.
</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    <strong>People who already asked you to do this for free.</strong> They have
    already told you it is worth having. Start here.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Anyone who already turns this work away.</strong> The tutor with a
    waiting list, the photographer who is booked, the gardener who will not do
    small jobs. Sending you the work they do not want costs them nothing and
    makes them look helpful.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The problem you can physically see.</strong> A garden that has got
    away from someone. A shop with photos taken in bad light. You are not
    guessing whether they need it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The room your customers are already in.</strong> A club, a
    noticeboard, a local group. Not the biggest room. The right one.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  Notice that none of these is posting online and waiting. A post reaches people
  who were not looking for you, which is the hardest possible sale. A message to
  someone with the problem is the easiest.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">What to say</h2>

<p>
  One sentence, then stop talking. What you do, what it costs, when you could
  start. Send a link so they can just buy instead of having to reply.
</p>

<p>
  Do not apologize for the price. Do not explain that you are young. Do not offer
  a discount nobody asked for. The most common way a first conversation is lost
  is by carrying on talking past the moment the other person had already decided
  yes.
</p>

<p>
  Expect most people to say no or say nothing. That is the normal ratio, not a
  verdict on you. If one in ten says yes and you talk to thirty people, you have
  three customers and you have learned what the other twenty seven objected to,
  which is worth more.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Doing one job free, on purpose</h2>

<p>
  Doing one job free to have something to show is a fair trade, with two rules.
  Decide in advance that it is exactly one. And ask for something back:
  permission to show the work, and a sentence you can quote. Those are worth more
  right now than the payment would have been.
</p>

<p>
  "I will do the next one cheap as well" is not a strategy. It is a habit, and it
  is difficult to stop once customers expect it.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Then find the thing that repeats</h2>

<p>
  Once you have five customers, ask each of them one question: how did you end up
  hearing about me? One or two answers will come up more than the rest. That is
  your channel, and your job from then on is to do more of that specific thing
  instead of a bit of everything.
</p>

<p>
  Most small businesses find that the answer is a recommendation from somebody
  who used them before. Which is a lesson in itself: the fastest way to get new
  customers is usually to be very good to the ones you have.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    <strong>Ask at the right moment.</strong> The best time to ask for a
    recommendation is the minute you finish a job, when the person is happiest,
    not a week later. Ask for something specific. "Do you know one other person
    who might want this?" works. "Tell your friends" does not.
  </p>
</div>
